<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New heart failure in an active 85-year-old woman, likely with preserved ejection fraction. Please confirm the diagnosis, review the echocardiogram, and advise on any specific cause and on therapy. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks; echocardiogram booked for the same morning. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Does the echocardiogram confirm heart failure with preserved ejection fraction?</item><item>Is there any sign of a specific cause, such as cardiac amyloidosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, or significant valve disease, that would change treatment?</item><item>Do you agree with adding spironolactone once potassium and kidney function are stable?</item><item>Is cardiac rehabilitation or any further testing worthwhile at her level of activity?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Miss Anthony is seen at the request of Dr. Pemberton two weeks after presenting with exertional breathlessness, orthopnea, and ankle swelling. On furosemide and empagliflozin she is already less breathless, and her home weight has fallen by about 1.7 kg. She has had no chest pain, palpitations, syncope, or symptoms of carpal tunnel syndrome, numbness, or orthostatic lightheadedness. She lectures and travels most months and wants to keep doing so. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Susan B. Anthony was born on February 15, 1820, in Adams, Massachusetts, the second child of Daniel and Lucy Read Anthony. [D]</item><item>Anthony voted in the 1872 presidential election, was arrested at her Rochester home, and was convicted at Canandaigua, New York; she refused to pay the $100 fine. [D]</item><item>Her father, Daniel Anthony, who had not had a day&apos;s illness in forty years, was suddenly stricken in November 1862 with what was called acute neuralgia of the stomach and died two weeks later, on November 25, 1862, at 69. [D]</item><item>Her 1898 biography describes a long-lived family: her mother died at eighty-six, her paternal grandfather lived past ninety-seven, and both maternal grandparents died in their eighty-fourth year. [D]</item><item>In January 1891, after weeks of what seemed a persistent la grippe, Anthony collapsed at the Garrison home in Brookline, Massachusetts; pneumonia threatened and she kept to her room for a week. [D]</item><item>On July 26, 1895, near the end of an address at Lakeside, Ohio, Anthony stopped abruptly and fainted; from July 30 she spent a month in bed with what was called nervous prostration, described as her first serious illness in seventy-five years. [D]</item><item>Her biographer described her daily habits in the late 1890s: a cold sponge bath each morning, simple food, tea and coffee in moderation, a rest after meals and an hour&apos;s rest each afternoon, and fresh air and exercise every day, walking from the station after a day on the train. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

