<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (I50.32). Confirmed. Ejection fraction 62 percent with concentric remodeling, left atrial volume index 42 mL/m², and average E/e&apos; 16 meet the diagnostic criteria with her raised NT-proBNP. Hypertension is the most likely cause. [S]</item><item>Specific causes. Cardiac amyloidosis is unlikely: septal thickness is only 11 mm, the ECG shows high rather than low voltage, and she has no carpal tunnel syndrome, neuropathy, or spinal stenosis. There is no hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or significant valve disease. [S]</item><item>Hypertensive heart disease (I11.0). Blood pressure 146/80 mmHg remains above the 130/80 target and is the main treatable driver. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Today&apos;s transthoracic echocardiogram, the intake ECG, NT-proBNP, and both basic metabolic panels. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Continue empagliflozin 10 mg daily long term. [S]</item><item>Start spironolactone 12.5 mg daily now; potassium 3.9 and eGFR 58 allow it. Check potassium and creatinine in one to two weeks. [S]</item><item>Continue furosemide 20 mg daily until euvolemic, then change to as-needed use guided by daily weights. [S]</item><item>No amyloid imaging or light-chain testing unless new red flags appear. [S]</item><item>No repeat echocardiogram unless her clinical status changes. [S]</item><item>Cardiac rehabilitation if her exercise tolerance plateaus; for now continue daily walking. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Pemberton by telephone today; the patient agrees with the plan. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care will manage the titration and monitoring on the CMID schedule. Cardiology will see her again for unexplained deterioration, a hospital admission, or blood pressure above target despite two more agents. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>
